					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606661223" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1425835727 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Mar 2015 17:28</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2247207">
									

<p>I'm no-voting, but leaning towards down, purely for clarity issues. See below:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Side points first:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Accidental civilian observation of SCP-2086 instances engaging in this activity is minimized due to the significantly smaller size of juvenile specimens.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't buy this. I mean, we're still talking about bus route signs in major towns and cities being stolen by cars using wheels as limbs. I'd find it more credible if, perhaps, you specified that juveniles are nocturnal? I don't live in a city, though, so take it with a grain of salt.</p>
<p>Also, style wise, I find the paragraph describing the human corpse decoy and the final paragraph about how it is made either unnecessarily repetitive or oddly split up. It'd be nice if there was a way to shift those together.</p>
<p>On to the big clarity issues:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A newborn specimen of SCP-2086 can be expected to grow to maturity within one week.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Mature instances of SCP-2086 do not feed, instead living off of nutrients consumed as juveniles.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Each passenger is then forcefully removed from the mature instance by a juvenile&#8230;. (<em>Feeding and preservation process</em>)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>1: Is that a week to reach juvenile size or full maturity? If juvenile, how long does it take a juvenile instance to become a mature one? What is the typical lifespan of a 2086?<br />
2: Does a juvenile only ever need to eat one corpse? If not, how many more corpses does it need before becoming mature, and what does it do with them?</p>
<p>Basically, if your intention that these things die off like mayflies, breed like crazy, and only need one human corpse per instance, I dig it. That gives it a frenetic intensity and a potential to snowball that earns the Keter.</p>
<p>As written, you're just a few hard data points short of getting that across. Specify a lifespan and the one corpse thing (and maybe some birth rates or gestation times if you want to get really fancy), and I would love this to pieces.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

